>>>Why is it that sometimes when you read a speelbook your knowledge
>>>of the speel becomes keener (what does this really mean?) and
>>
>>I *think* that it takes less magical energy to cast it. Either that,
>>or you can cast it more times before you have to re-read from your
>>spellbook (if you still have it - see following)
>>
>	After you cast a spell enough, when you attempt to cast it more
>you will only get "coursing magical energy twist through you" and you
>automatically fail the spell attempt.  To renew your knowledge of the
>spell you have to read it again; after several readings the book 
>becomes too faint to read.  Whether reading it before this time does
>you any good I'm not sure about; I would bet, though, that it only
>takes you back to where you started, which is to say you won't get
>any benefit by reading it twice in a row.

Each time you read a spellbook, you gain 1..(9-lev) uses of it (for a level
1 spell 1..8 uses, level 7 spell it would only be 1..2).  If currently have
less than (11-lev) uses of a given spell, and you read it again, you gain
an additional 1..(9-lev) uses.

So the bottom line is you can always read a spellbook twice, and gain more
uses of it.  Depending on the random number generator, you may be able to
read it several times, and each time learn it better (and exercise your
wisdom).  As soon as you get the message "You know that spell quite well
already", then you know the spell as well as you can.  This final message
doesn't make the spellbook any fainter, and doesn't make you learn it any
better (but if it's already fairly faded, you may burn it out).  So again,
read it twice, but not more unless you're feeling lucky.
